    <!--
      Sakinah frosted-glass surface system.

      Five tokens, one job each. Depth comes from the real window blur
      (helper/GlassBlur.kt) plus one step of tint between levels — not from painted
      highlights, gradients or shadows.

      An earlier iteration grew to twenty tokens (inner shade falloff bands, recess
      floor gradients, thumb contact/ambient shadows, rim lights). Every one of them
      measured correctly and still read as embossed plastic, because a shape drawable
      cannot blur and a hard-edged band is a border however it is stacked. They are gone.

      LEVELS, darkest surface to brightest ink:
        glassScrim   - the wallpaper dim a full-screen panel sits on
        glassSunken  - a well cut into the panel (segment track, input rows)
        glassRaised  - a card lifted off the panel
        glassActive  - a selected fill; ink on it MUST flip (see GlassInk.kt)
        glassHairline- the edge that defines any of the above
    -->
    <attr name="glassScrim" format="color" />
    <attr name="glassSunken" format="color" />
    <attr name="glassRaised" format="color" />
    <attr name="glassActive" format="color" />
    <attr name="glassHairline" format="color" />

    <!--
      HOME WASH — a separate ramp from glassScrim, on purpose.

      The panel scrim exists to turn the wallpaper into a canvas you can put a whole
      sheet of glass on, so it is heavy and flat. The home screen has no panel: the
      clock, date and app labels are drawn straight onto the user's wallpaper, and the
      wallpaper is the point of the screen. Reusing the panel scrim there crushed the
      lower two thirds of the wallpaper to black for no benefit — nothing down there
      needed that much help.

      So: two surfaces, not one ramp. A band sized to the clock block carries the clock
      and date; a flat, very light floor carries the app labels in the middle of the
      screen. Trying to serve both from one full-height gradient does not work — a linear
      gradient's middle stop is pinned to the middle of the VIEW, so a band strong enough
      for the clock dims the entire top half. See drawable/bg_home_wash.xml.

      The floor is deliberately not transparent (labels measured 2.99:1 with the wash
      faded out) but at 20% it barely touches the wallpaper; @style/TextHomeLabel's halo
      does the rest.
    -->
    <attr name="homeWashTop" format="color" />
    <attr name="homeWashFloor" format="color" />
</resources>
